ns. Compare their lengths and offsets with interior markers.\n- For sparse markers, inspect row/column projections, rays to nearest edges, translated or periodic copies, and whether source cells are recolored after drawing.\n- Compare horizontal and vertical examples for rotation-invariant behavior.\n- Determine whether fill stops before a boundary, replaces markers, or passes through them.\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- A single demo can support multiple fill directions or extents; use cross-example evidence.\n- Do not confuse a ray from a marker with a full projected stripe. Inspect exact output cells.\n- A plausible-looking grid is not verified until the same rule reproduces all demos.\n- Avoid hand-counting large output grids without confirming dimensions and coordinates.\n\n## Reference notes\n\nSee `references/border-band-projection-case.md` for an explicitly provisional analysis of paired edge bands and interior markers. It is an example, not a universal rule.\n", "path": "puzzles/arc-grid-reasoning/SKILL.md", "skill_dir": "/home/USER

